mpt shows connection refused, confirming the node was actually down mid-reboot.\"},{\"requirement\":\"Proof of changed boot identity after restart\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-f1e194cd96334283\"],\"summary\":\"Exec access restored with new boot_id 6523512c-7e09-44bf-a9c7-4ff3135ac8b5, differing from the pre-restart boot_id, proving an actual reboot occurred.\"},{\"requirement\":\"Post-restart recovery of required data and filesystem mount\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-ae6f2757a3e2422a\"],\"summary\":\"Confirmed /srv/data remounted via fstab post-reboot with identical file listing and identical sha256 checksums to the pre-modification baseline.\"},{\"requirement\":\"Post-restart snapshot/subvolume integrity\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-05523839ed6e44fe\"],\"summary\":\"Listed btrfs subvolumes after reboot showing current, snap_baseline, and corrupted-history subvolumes all intact for continued recoverability.\"}]}","structured_output":{"status":"completed","summary":"On node1, /srv/data is now a btrfs subvolume (\"current\") on a loopback-backed btrfs filesystem (/var/lib/srvdata/backing.img), mounted persistently via /etc/fstab. Populated it with application data, took a read-only snapshot (snap_baseline), then modified/corrupted the live directory (overwrote a file, deleted another, added a stray file). Restored /srv/data to the exact snapshot content using the btrfs subvolume-swap technique (demoted the corrupted subvolume, promoted a fresh writable snapshot of snap_baseline as the new \"current\"), and verified byte-for-byte checksum equality with the pre-modification baseline. Completed the mandatory single-node restart protocol: captured boot_id and data-health baseline, issued `sudo reboot`, polled until SSH/exec access returned, confirmed boot_id changed, and verified /srv/data remounted via fstab with identical file content/checksums and all btrfs subvolumes (current, snap_baseline, corrupted history) intact post-restart.","actions_performed":["Inspected node1 OS/filesys